# Local development environment. Copy this file to backend/.env and fill it in:
#
#    cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
#
# backend/.env is gitignored and is read at boot by dotenv-rails. This file --
# the .example -- is committed, so it is the one place the variable names are
# written down. Never put a real value in it.
#
# These are the Cloudflare R2 credentials used to store feedback screenshots.
# Leave them blank and nothing breaks: the backend only selects the R2 service
# when R2_BUCKET is set, and otherwise falls back to local disk storage. That is
# the right state for CI and for a fresh clone.
#
# Where the values come from: Cloudflare dashboard -> R2 -> Manage API tokens
# -> Create API token, "Object Read & Write", scoped to the wend buckets. The
# full walkthrough is doc/how-to/cloud-storage.md.

# The S3 API endpoint, copied whole from the R2 dashboard -- bucket -> Settings
# -> S3 API, or the page shown just after a token is created. It looks like
#   https://<32 hex characters>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# Paste it rather than assembling it. Cloudflare serves a certificate per
# account and chooses it by hostname, so an account it does not recognise
# refuses the TLS handshake -- which surfaces as "SSL alert number 40" and says
# nothing about which value was wrong. If you have more than one Cloudflare
# account, note that a *user* API token spans all of them, so the account shown
# beside the token is not necessarily the one holding the bucket.
R2_ENDPOINT=

# Older alternative to R2_ENDPOINT: the bare account id, from which the endpoint
# above is assembled. Set one or the other; R2_ENDPOINT wins if both are set.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=

# The R2 API token's access key id and secret. The secret is shown once, when
# the token is created -- if you lost it, roll the token and re-issue.
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Development uses its own bucket, so what you upload while poking at the app
# never lands in the bucket staging is showing people.
R2_BUCKET=wend-feedback-localhost
